{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-78628469-ddc3-43b8-92b4-99ebf976aa1d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b0e42a2b3a7f5c3c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9008-jsho0m0a/artifacts/epic-reviews/RHAISTRAT-9008-decomp-review.md","content":"# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9008\n\n## Review Summary\n\nScore: 13/14 — pass\nRecommendation: accept\n\nThe decomposition correctly translates a vague, contradiction-laden strategy\n(three unprioritized HLRs, three open questions, contradictory account\nfeedback) into a clean 6-epic main-plan DAG with two conditional branches.\nCoverage, DAG consistency, boundary discipline, signal scoring, and\nrule-mandated acceptance criteria all check out. One gating-metadata gap on\nthe docs-authoring epic (rubric level-1 defect) and two minor observations\nare recorded below; none change execution order or epic existence.\n\n## Criterion Details\n\n### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n\nAll four HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix is\npresent and accurate:\n\n- HLR-1 (admin simplification vs. fine-grained controls) → E001\n  (Investigation) + BRANCH-A-E007 / BRANCH-B-E007 (conditional\n  implementations). Both branch files trace HLR-1 in their HLR Traceability\n  sections.\n- HLR-2 (onboarding < 1 day) → E002 (Investigation) + E004 (Implementation).\n- HLR-3 (enterprise tooling integration) → E003 (Investigation) + E005\n  (Implementation).\n- HLR-4 (whitepaper, P2) → E006 (docs-authoring).\n\nPriority handling is sound. The strategy leaves HLR-1..HLR-3 unprioritized;\nthe decomposer defaulted them to P1 with an explicit health warning — a\nreasonable, documented judgment, not a coverage error. Priority inheritance\nholds: E001/E002/E003 (P1) block P1 implementations; the branch epics are\nP1 matching the HLR-1 work they enable. E006's P2 is correctly derived from\nthe strategy's `priority: Normal` via the docs-authoring exception, and its\nP2 does not propagate upstream. No epic maps to HLRs at multiple priority\nlevels, so no priority-collapse risk exists. No P0 HLRs exist in the\nstrategy.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nCross-checked every edge in the summary diagram against epic frontmatter\n`dependencies` — all 9 edges match exactly, in both directions:\n\n- E001 → BRANCH-A-E007, E001 → BRANCH-B-E007 (both branch files list E001 in\n  `dependencies`)\n- E002 → E004, E003 → E005 (frontmatter matches)\n- E001 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 (E006's dependencies list all\n  three)\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent as required. No cycles, no edges\nto nonexistent epics. Branch files set both `branch` (A/B, matching\nfilenames) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9008-E001`, with the gated epic also in\n`dependencies` — the pipeline will not silently drop them. No main-plan epic\nlists a branch epic in its dependencies; E006 correctly depends on the\nInvestigation E001 rather than a branch outcome, per the sanctioned pattern\nfor main-plan work that hinges on a gated outcome.\n\nParallelism is correct: the three Investigations run in parallel (distinct\nunknowns, distinct deliverables — not combinable under Step 3's\nsame-experiment rule), and E004 ∥ E005 are parallel (different\ncomponents/teams/repos, no shared artifacts). No konflux-onboarding,\nrepo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist — architecture context\nconfirms both Dashboard and Notebooks are GA and Konflux-onboarded — so\nRules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints and the Rule 25 build-pipeline AC are\ncorrectly not applicable, and the summary documents this explicitly.\n\nNo content-discoverable data/artifact dependency is missing from the DAG:\nE004 consumes E002's baseline/target (edge present), E005 consumes E003's\nshortlist/surface mapping (edge present), E006 consumes E001's decision and\nE004/E005's outcomes (edges present), branches consume E001's enumerated\nscope (edges present). Critical path length 3 (E002→E004→E006 and\nE003→E005→E006) matches the frontmatter value and is within the M-standard\nheuristic (3-4).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEvery epic's component/team tuple matches architecture context: Dashboard →\nRHOAI Dashboard (E001, E003, E005, both branches), Notebooks → Workbenches\n(E002, E004). E006 uses the canonical \"Documentation\" component from\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`; its \"RHOAI Documentation\" team is inferred\nbecause the strategy has no Impacted Teams table — this is disclosed as a\nhealth warning, which is the correct handling for a missing section.\n\nNo scope duplication: no two epics independently build the same data\nstructure, UI surface, or API. The Investigation epics produce decision\nrecords/baselines/shortlists; the Implementations consume them — no overlap.\nNo work-product type mixing: the whitepaper (content authoring) is a\nseparate docs-authoring epic; no code epic bundles content authoring.\n\nMinor observation (not score-reducing): E004's scope hedges that console\n(Dashboard) surfaces may need coordinated changes \"where the baseline points\nthere,\" and E005's integration surfaces span console/auth/workbench/serving.\nBoth epics are component-attributed to a single team while their eventual\nscope is genuinely indeterminate until their gating Investigations resolve;\neach carries an explicit `rewrite` gate_failure_impact stating the epic will\nbe rewritten or split across owning components if the outcome lands\nelsewhere. This is a reasonable handling of undetermined scope rather than a\nboundary violation, and no epic is evidently oversized relative to what the\nstrategy specifies.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n\nTypes are correct throughout. Each Investigation genuinely changes\ndownstream structure: E001's outcome selects which branch implementation\nexists (bounded, 3 outcomes — two as branches, blend documented as a\nrewrite of the nearer branch); E002's outcome determines what E004 builds;\nE003's outcome determines whether E005 exists and what it integrates. None\nis an informational-only investigation misclassified. All Implementations\nproduce concrete artifacts. All `gated_by` targets appear in the\ncorresponding epic's direct `dependencies` (E004→E002, E005→E003,\nbranches→E001), and every non-null `gate_failure_impact` has both `action`\nand a substantive `fallback_approach`.\n\nOne gating-metadata defect, matching the rubric's level-1 description\n(\"an Investigation dependency missing `gated_by` on a downstream epic\"):\nE006 lists the Investigation E001 in `dependencies` but has `gated_by: null`.\nThe edge is documented in the summary as a Rule 11 extension — E001 standing\nin for the branch implementations that main-plan epics must not depend on —\nand E006 exists with the same structure under every E001 outcome (only its\nadmin-configuration content varies), so the null is arguably correct for a\nscheduling/content dependency. However, the summary itself calls E001 \"the\ngating Investigation\" for E006, and the decomposer contract (Step 8b)\nrequires `gated_by` on every epic depending on an Investigation, so\nautomated gate detection will not see this coupling. Recording as minor\nseverity: it does not change execution order (E006 still cannot start before\nE001 via `dependencies`) or epic existence.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal sets are type-appropriate everywhere: E001-E003 carry\n`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric) with `ai_signals: null`;\nE004-E006 and both branches carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) with\n`investigation_signals: null`. Rationale files exist for all eight epics and\nevery frontmatter value matches its rationale.\n\nSpot-checks against the rubrics: E001's `human_judgment_required: -2` is\ncorrect (the gating question — which contradictory account requirement wins\n— is itself a stakeholder decision); E001/E003's `source_accessibility: 0`\nis correct (the answers live in unconsolidated field feedback, not readable\nartifacts); E002's `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` correctly distinguishes\nthe peripheral baseline measurement (needs a live cluster) from the gating\nscope-definition question; E004/E005's `open_questions: -1` correctly\nreflects the gating unresolved questions; both branches' `pattern_precedent:\n1` and `architecture_claims: 1` are substantiated by the Dashboard\n(OdhDashboardConfig CR) and Notebooks (culling defaults with per-workbench\noverrides) architecture context files; E006's `human_process_gates: -1`\ncorrectly captures the docs-content review/sign-off process, and its\n`open_questions: 0` rationale (upstream uncertainty captured as DAG\ndependencies, not in-epic design decisions) is sound.\n\nOne borderline value: E002's `source_accessibility: 1` — the onboarding\njourney structure is determinable from readable artifacts, but the measured\ntime baseline is not (that part needs a cluster and observation). The\nrationale is honest about the split and the value is defensible for the\ngating question; this is the kind of arguable-but-reasonable call the rubric\npermits without penalty.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the HLR text and\nstrategy context. All rule-mandated ACs are present wherever the rule's\ntrigger condition holds:\n\n- Rule 23 (replacement/reshaping of existing capability → rollback /\n  feature-flag AC): BRANCH-A-E007 and BRANCH-B-E007 each carry an explicit\n  feature-flag/rollback AC (\"reverting restores the previously exposed\n  configuration surface\" / \"restores the prior platform-wide-only\n  configuration behavior\"); E004 carries a reversibility AC for changed\n  defaults; E005 carries an additive-behavior-preserved AC.\n- Rule 24 (docs-authoring → technically reviewed against implementation):\n  E006's second AC requires technical review \"against the implemented\n  platform behavior ... by someone with knowledge of the implementations,\n  with review comments resolved.\"\n- Rule 25 (konflux in dependency chain → build pipeline green): correctly\n  not triggered — no konflux-onboarding epic exists in any chain.\n\nACs are concretely testable where the strategy permits (e.g., E004's\n\"measured across the defined onboarding scope and with the same measurement\nmethod as the baseline, is under one day\"; E002's \"measured on a\nrepresentative cluster configuration rather than estimated\"). No epic body\nreferences sibling epics by draft ID — all cross-epic references use plain\nterms (\"the onboarding definition and baseline\", \"the direction decision\",\n\"the tooling identification work\"). Zero draft-ID references across all\neight epic bodies.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is accounted for. Each of the three strategy open\nquestions maps to exactly one Investigation epic (Q1→E001, Q2→E002, Q3→E003)\n— the \"genuine unknowns\" escape from triage was correctly applied since each\nanswer changes which epics exist or what they do. The problem statement's\nunconsolidated-feedback concern is carried as consolidation scope in E001\nand E003. \"Leadership wants visible usability progress this release\" is\ncarried forward as health warning 3 (investigation-heavy structure vs.\nrelease visibility). The empty out-of-scope section is flagged as a scope\ntrap (health warning 4) with mitigation via the rewrite gates on E004/E005.\nThe contradiction inside HLR-1 is handled by the bounded Investigation +\nconditional branches rather than an arbitrary pick, and all three outcomes\nare covered: simplification (branch A), granularity (branch B), and blend\n(documented as a rewrite of the nearer branch, with both branch files\nrecording `action=remove` for the non-selected outcome).\n\nHLR-4's whitepaper requirement has its dedicated docs-authoring epic (the\nstrategy has no Prerequisites & Process Gates section, so the\nDocumentation-Support trigger does not apply, but the explicit HLR makes the\nepic mandatory regardless — and it exists). No capability, acceptance\ncriterion, or stakeholder concern from the strategy is silently dropped.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":49794,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4fedbb549889118","agent_id":"a96fc96f9a143a395","session_id":"75f31b44-6829-4bc6-8c24-b6fae2a504e2","uuid":"9672e083-fa55-4939-b841-88fa9c18c5b0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T00:04:46.531Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
