{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-948fa35a09e93e46","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9008\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Type Correctness\n9\t  description: 'E006 lists Investigation E001 in dependencies but has gated_by: null.\n10\t    The edge is documented as a Rule 11 extension (E001 standing in for the branch\n11\t    implementations main-plan epics must not depend on) and E006 keeps its structure\n12\t    under every E001 outcome, but the summary calls E001 the gating Investigation\n13\t    for the whitepaper content; setting gated_by=E001 with action=rewrite (admin-configuration\n14\t    guidance rewritten to the resolved direction) would make the coupling visible\n15\t    to automated gate detection. Rubric level-1 gating-metadata defect; no execution-order\n16\t    impact.'\n17\t- severity: minor\n18\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n19\t  description: 'E004 and E005 scope hedges cross-component surfaces (E004: console\n20\t    surfaces a new data scientist traverses; E005: console/auth/workbench/serving\n21\t    integration surfaces) while each epic is attributed to a single component/team.\n22\t    Mitigated by explicit rewrite gate_failure_impact (epic rewritten or split across\n23\t    owning components if the gating investigation lands elsewhere); acceptable handling\n24\t    of indeterminate scope, but the final scope assignment should be re-checked when\n25\t    the investigations resolve.'\n26\t- severity: minor\n27\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n28\t  description: 'E002 source_accessibility: 1 is borderline: the onboarding journey\n29\t    structure is determinable from readable artifacts, but the measured per-stage\n30\t    time baseline (a stated deliverable) requires a live cluster and observation,\n31\t    not artifacts. Rationale is honest about the split; value is defensible for the\n32\t    gating scope-definition question.'\n33\terror: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9008\n36\t\n37\t## Review Summary\n38\t\n39\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n40\tRecommendation: accept\n41\t\n42\tThe decomposition correctly translates a vague, contradiction-laden strategy\n43\t(three unprioritized HLRs, three open questions, contradictory account\n44\tfeedback) into a clean 6-epic main-plan DAG with two conditional branches.\n45\tCoverage, DAG consistency, boundary discipline, signal scoring, and\n46\trule-mandated acceptance criteria all check out. One gating-metadata gap on\n47\tthe docs-authoring epic (rubric level-1 defect) and two minor observations\n48\tare recorded below; none change execution order or epic existence.\n49\t\n50\t## Criterion Details\n51\t\n52\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n53\t\n54\tAll four HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix is\n55\tpresent and accurate:\n56\t\n57\t- HLR-1 (admin simplification vs. fine-grained controls) → E001\n58\t  (Investigation) + BRANCH-A-E007 / BRANCH-B-E007 (conditional\n59\t  implementations). Both branch files trace HLR-1 in their HLR Traceability\n60\t  sections.\n61\t- HLR-2 (onboarding < 1 day) → E002 (Investigation) + E004 (Implementation).\n62\t- HLR-3 (enterprise tooling integration) → E003 (Investigation) + E005\n63\t  (Implementation).\n64\t- HLR-4 (whitepaper, P2) → E006 (docs-authoring).\n65\t\n66\tPriority handling is sound. The strategy leaves HLR-1..HLR-3 unprioritized;\n67\tthe decomposer defaulted them to P1 with an explicit health warning — a\n68\treasonable, documented judgment, not a coverage error. Priority inheritance\n69\tholds: E001/E002/E003 (P1) block P1 implementations; the branch epics are\n70\tP1 matching the HLR-1 work they enable. E006's P2 is correctly derived from\n71\tthe strategy's `priority: Normal` via the docs-authoring exception, and its\n72\tP2 does not propagate upstream. No epic maps to HLRs at multiple priority\n73\tlevels, so no priority-collapse risk exists. No P0 HLRs exist in the\n74\tstrategy.\n75\t\n76\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n77\t\n78\tCross-checked every edge in the summary diagram against epic frontmatter\n79\t`dependencies` — all 9 edges match exactly, in both directions:\n80\t\n81\t- E001 → BRANCH-A-E007, E001 → BRANCH-B-E007 (both branch files list E001 in\n82\t  `dependencies`)\n83\t- E002 → E004, E003 → E005 (frontmatter matches)\n84\t- E001 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 (E006's dependencies list all\n85\t  three)\n86\t\n87\tArrows point from dependency to dependent as required. No cycles, no edges\n88\tto nonexistent epics. Branch files set both `branch` (A/B, matching\n89\tfilenames) and `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-9008-E001`, with the gated epic also in\n90\t`dependencies` — the pipeline will not silently drop them. No main-plan epic\n91\tlists a branch epic in its dependencies; E006 correctly depends on the\n92\tInvestigation E001 rather than a branch outcome, per the sanctioned pattern\n93\tfor main-plan work that hinges on a gated outcome.\n94\t\n95\tParallelism is correct: the three Investigations run in parallel (distinct\n96\tunknowns, distinct deliverables — not combinable under Step 3's\n97\tsame-experiment rule), and E004 ∥ E005 are parallel (different\n98\tcomponents/teams/repos, no shared artifacts). No konflux-onboarding,\n99\trepo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist — architecture context\n100\tconfirms both Dashboard and Notebooks are GA and Konflux-onboarded — so\n101\tRules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints and the Rule 25 build-pipeline AC are\n102\tcorrectly not applicable, and the summary documents this explicitly.\n103\t\n104\tNo content-discoverable data/artifact dependency is missing from the DAG:\n105\tE004 consumes E002's baseline/target (edge present), E005 consumes E003's\n106\tshortlist/surface mapping (edge present), E006 consumes E001's decision and\n107\tE004/E005's outcomes (edges present), branches consume E001's enumerated\n108\tscope (edges present). Critical path length 3 (E002→E004→E006 and\n109\tE003→E005→E006) matches the frontmatter value and is within the M-standard\n110\theuristic (3-4).\n111\t\n112\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n113\t\n114\tEvery epic's component/team tuple matches architecture context: Dashboard →\n115\tRHOAI Dashboard (E001, E003, E005, both branches), Notebooks → Workbenches\n116\t(E002, E004). E006 uses the canonical \"Documentation\" component from\n117\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`; its \"RHOAI Documentation\" team is inferred\n118\tbecause the strategy has no Impacted Teams table — this is disclosed as a\n119\thealth warning, which is the correct handling for a missing section.\n120\t\n121\tNo scope duplication: no two epics independently build the same data\n122\tstructure, UI surface, or API. The Investigation epics produce decision\n123\trecords/baselines/shortlists; the Implementations consume them — no overlap.\n124\tNo work-product type mixing: the whitepaper (content authoring) is a\n125\tseparate docs-authoring epic; no code epic bundles content authoring.\n126\t\n127\tMinor observation (not score-reducing): E004's scope hedges that console\n128\t(Dashboard) surfaces may need coordinated changes \"where the baseline points\n129\tthere,\" and E005's integration surfaces span console/auth/workbench/serving.\n130\tBoth epics are component-attributed to a single team while their eventual\n131\tscope is genuinely indeterminate until their gating Investigations resolve;\n132\teach carries an explicit `rewrite` gate_failure_impact stating the epic will\n133\tbe rewritten or split across owning components if the outcome lands\n134\telsewhere. This is a reasonable handling of undetermined scope rather than a\n135\tboundary violation, and no epic is evidently oversized relative to what the\n136\tstrategy specifies.\n137\t\n138\t### 4. Type Correctness (1/2)\n139\t\n140\tTypes are correct throughout. Each Investigation genuinely changes\n141\tdownstream structure: E001's