E004/E005. No priority collapse: no epic bundles HLRs\nat multiple priority levels. `triage` is null (full decomposition), so\nmulti-epic output is expected.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nDiagram ↔ frontmatter cross-check: every edge in the summary diagram\n(E001→BRANCH-A-E007, E001→BRANCH-B-E007, E002→E004, E003→E005, E001→E006,\nE004→E006, E005→E006) has a matching entry in the target epic's\n`dependencies`, and vice versa; no frontmatter dependency references a\nnonexistent epic. Arrows point from dependency to dependent as the\nconvention requires. No cycles.\n\nEdge justification: the three Investigations are correctly parallel (rules\n14/15); E004 ∥ E005 are correctly parallel (different components, teams,\nrepos, no shared artifacts). No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or\nlicense-validation epics exist (both components are Konflux-onboarded per\narchitecture context), so rules 7/8/10 are not triggered and no Rule 25 AC\nis required. The E001→E006 edge (Rule 11 extension) is justified: the\nwhitepaper content genuinely consumes E001's decision record, and since\nmain-plan epics must not depend on branch epics, depending on the gating\nInvestigation is the correct expression of that data dependency. It\nserializes nothing (E006 already waits on E004/E005, which wait on E002/E003\nrunning in parallel with E001).\n\nBranch wiring: both branch files set `branch` (matching filename letters)\nand `gated_by=RHAISTRAT-9008-E001`, and E001 appears in each branch's\n`dependencies`. No main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its `dependencies`\n(E006 correctly depends on E001, not on the branches). Critical path length\n3 (Investigation → Implementation → docs) is within the M-standard heuristic\n(3-4) for this strategy size. Content scan for uncaptured data dependencies\nfound none — E004/E005/E006/branch consumption of upstream deliverables all\nhas corresponding edges.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEvery epic carries a single canonical component (all present in\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`, including \"Documentation\" for E006) and a\nsingle team matching the architecture-context owning teams (RHOAI Dashboard,\nWorkbenches). No epic bundles multiple teams. No work-product type mixing:\nE006 is pure docs-authoring; all other Implementations are code. No scope\nduplication across siblings: E001 and E003 both consolidate field feedback\nbut resolve different unknowns with different deliverables (direction\ndecision vs. tool shortlist) — the decompose rules explicitly permit\nmultiple Investigations per component/team.\n\nTwo minor observations, both mitigated by explicit gates:\n- E004 (Notebooks/Workbenches) hedges in scope into \"coordinated changes to\n  the console surfaces\" (Dashboard) if the baseline points there. Its\n  `gate_failure_impact` explicitly commits to re-checking/splitting the\n  component and team assignment when the onboarding definition resolves, so\n  the boundary ambiguity is gated rather than silently crossed.\n- E005 bundles all shortlisted integrations into one epic regardless of\n  count or surface; if the shortlist spans components, this could exceed a\n  sprint. Again explicitly handled by the rewrite/split gate.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll three Investigations are genuine: each maps to a strategy open question\nwhose answer changes which downstream epics exist or what they do (E001\nselects the branch; E002's fallback explicitly contemplates E004's removal;\nE003's fallback contemplates E005's removal), and each has downstream\ndependents. All Implementations produce concrete artifacts. `gated_by` /\n`gate_failure_impact` metadata is complete and correct: E004 (gated_by E002,\nrewrite + fallback), E005 (gated_by E003, rewrite + fallback), E006 (gated_by\nE001, rewrite + fallback), both branches (gated_by E001, remove + fallback).\nEvery `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. Every\nepic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set. E006's\ngate on E001 is a true gate, not a mere scheduling dependency: a different\nE001 outcome rewrites the whitepaper's admin-configuration guidance, so\n`action=rewrite` is accurate.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal-set selection is correct: the three Investigations carry\n`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric); all five Implementations\n(including both branches) carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). Frontmatter\nvalues match the rationale files exactly in all eight cases.\n\nSpot-checks against the rubric and architecture context: E001's\n`human_judgment_required: -2` is right (the gating precedence question is a\nstakeholder decision); E001/E003 `source_accessibility: 0` is right (answers\nlive in unconsolidated account feedback); branch epics' `pattern_precedent:\n1` claims are substantiated by the architecture context (OdhDashboardConfig\nadmin surfaces; culling defaults with per-workbench overrides); E004/E005\n`change_specificity: -1` / `open_questions: -1` correctly reflect the\nstrategy's undefined scope. One borderline value: E002's\n`source_accessibility: +1` — the gating scope-definition question is partly\na stakeholder decision (acknowledged by `human_judgment_required: -1` in the\nsame file), but the rationale carefully scopes the signal to the\nartifact-determinable current-state journey and defers the measured baseline\nto `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1`. Defensible, recorded as a minor note.\nPer instructions, `ai_implementability` totals were not checked\n(pipeline-computed).\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text and investigation\ndeliverables (e.g., E004's \"measured across the defined onboarding scope and\nwith the same measurement method as the baseline\" is properly operational).\nRule-mandated ACs are all present where applicable: Rule 23 rollback /\nfeature-flag ACs on BRANCH-A-E007 and BRANCH-B-E007 (both reshape the\nexisting admin configuration surface) and reversibility ACs on E004 (default\nbehavior changes) and E005 (additive integrations); Rule 24 technical-review\nAC on E006; Rule 25 not triggered (no konflux chain). No epic body\nreferences sibling epics by draft ID — all cross-epic references use plain\nterms (\"the onboarding-scope definition and baseline work\", \"the tooling\nidentification work\", \"the direction decision\").\n\nOne minor observation: E006's technical-review AC says content is reviewed\nagainst \"admin configuration direction as delivered\", but no ordering\nrelationship between E006 and the branch implementations exists — correctly\nso, since main-plan epics must not depend on branch epics. If the whitepaper\ncompletes before the selected branch lands, that AC is unsatisfiable as\nworded; it should reference the resolved direction (as E006's third AC and\nscope already do) rather than delivered implementation.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is accounted for: all four HLRs (criterion 1), all three\nopen questions carried forward as the three Investigations, the problem\nstatement's unconsolidated-feedback context reflected in E001/E003\nconsolidation scope, both affected components represented, and the\nempty out-of-scope captured as health warning 4 rather than ignored.\nStrategy-level context is not silently dropped: the leadership\nvisible-progress tension is explicitly recorded as health warning 3, and the\nmissing strategy sections as health warning 1. E006 satisfies the\ndocs-authoring requirement implied by HLR-4 (the strategy has no\nPrerequisites & Process Gates section, so no further docs obligation\nexists). The E001 → E006 edge ensures all three investigation outcomes reach\nthe whitepaper transitively or directly.\n\nConditional branches cover the bounded outcomes: branch A (simplification)\nand branch B (fine-grained controls) exist with full artifact contracts\n(branch, gated_by, gate_failure_impact, ai-signals files). The third\nenumerated outcome — a blended approach — is handled in summary prose as a\n`rewrite` of the closer branch. Minor observation: the branch files'\n`gate_failure_impact` fallbacks only cover blends \"dominated by\" the other\noutcome, so a balanced blend is addressed only in the decomposition summary,\nnot in the branch frontmatter; the outcome is nevertheless documented and\nnot silently dropped.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":52428,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b23e8a884f0e9d9","agent_id":"a43c4a9c68828c81a","session_id":"75f31b44-6829-4bc6-8c24-b6fae2a504e2","uuid":"853ff726-b61a-4ef2-88a5-c63ecf5a161e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T00:13:46.921Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9008 decomposition"}
